{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ca9GJhBnLtncvvR28Dxpib","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C01/KP(1/5)\n4\t**Competency Unit:** C01 — Body Consultation / Konsultasi Badan\n5\t**Work Activity 1:** Prepare Body Consultation Work Area\n6\t**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Objectives (Objektif)\n11\t\n12\tUpon completion of this information sheet, trainees shall be able to:\n13\t1. Explain the hygiene and safety practices required for a body consultation service area.\n14\t2. Identify the tools, equipment and materials used in body consultation.\n15\t3. Describe the proper arrangement and setup of a body consultation work area.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 1.0 Introduction (Pengenalan)\n20\t\n21\tBody consultation is the foundation of all aesthetic services. Before any treatment can be recommended or performed, a thorough body consultation must be conducted in a properly prepared work area. The work area must comply with hygiene and safety standards to ensure both client comfort and therapist safety.\n22\t\n23\tA well-prepared consultation area reflects professionalism and instills confidence in the client. The aesthetician (*pakar estetik*) must ensure the area is clean, organised, and equipped with all necessary tools before the client arrives.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## 2.0 Hygiene and Safety Practices (Amalan Kebersihan dan Keselamatan)\n28\t\n29\t> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Disinfectant solutions and chemical sanitisers (*larutan penyahjangkit*) must be used in a well-ventilated area. Never mix different disinfectant products — chemical reactions may release toxic fumes. Wear disposable gloves (*sarung tangan pakai buang*) when handling cleaning chemicals and change gloves between clients to prevent cross-contamination (*jangkitan silang*).\n30\t\n31\t### 2.1 Salon Hygiene Standards\n32\t\n33\tHygiene (*kebersihan*) in the salon is governed by:\n34\t- **Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (OSHA)** — Akta Keselamatan dan Kesihatan Pekerjaan 1994\n35\t- **Local authority regulations** — Peraturan pihak berkuasa tempatan\n36\t- **Ministry of Health guidelines** — Garis panduan Kementerian Kesihatan\n37\t\n38\tKey hygiene practices include:\n39\t\n40\t| Practice | Description |\n41\t|----------|-------------|\n42\t| Surface disinfection (*penyahjangkitan permukaan*) | All surfaces wiped with approved disinfectant before and after each client |\n43\t| Hand hygiene (*kebersihan tangan*) | Hand washing with antibacterial soap; use of hand sanitiser |\n44\t| Linen management (*pengurusan linen*) | Fresh towels, gowns and sheets for each client; soiled linen in covered bins |\n45\t| Air quality (*kualiti udara*) | Adequate ventilation; air purifier if required |\n46\t| Floor cleanliness (*kebersihan lantai*) | Swept and mopped daily; no debris or spillage |\n47\t\n48\t### 2.2 Safety Practices\n49\t\n50\t- Ensure no wet floors or tripping hazards in the consultation area.\n51\t- Electrical equipment must be PAT (Portable Appliance Testing) compliant.\n52\t- Fire extinguisher accessible within the salon premises.\n53\t- Emergency exits clearly marked.\n54\t- First aid kit (*peti pertolongan cemas*) available and stocked.\n55\t\n56\t---\n57\t\n58\t## 3.0 Tools, Equipment and Materials (Peralatan, Kelengkapan dan Bahan)\n59\t\n60\t### 3.1 Tools (Peralatan)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"42a7a5d3-7639-474f-ae97-9b2bcf9e2446","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:32:59.385Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C01\\KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C01/KP(1/5)\n**Competency Unit:** C01 — Body Consultation / Konsultasi Badan\n**Work Activity 1:** Prepare Body Consultation Work Area\n**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n\n---\n\n## Objectives (Objektif)\n\nUpon completion of this information sheet, trainees shall be able to:\n1. Explain the hygiene and safety practices required for a body consultation service area.\n2. Identify the tools, equipment and materials used in body consultation.\n3. Describe the proper arrangement and setup of a body consultation work area.\n\n---\n\n## 1.0 Introduction (Pengenalan)\n\nBody consultation is the foundation of all aesthetic services. Before any treatment can be recommended or performed, a thorough body consultation must be conducted in a properly prepared work area. The work area must comply with hygiene and safety standards to ensure both client comfort and therapist safety.\n\nA well-prepared consultation area reflects professionalism and instills confidence in the client. The aesthetician (*pakar estetik*) must ensure the area is clean, organised, and equipped with all necessary tools before the client arrives.\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 Hygiene and Safety Practices (Amalan Kebersihan dan Keselamatan)\n\n> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Disinfectant solutions and chemical sanitisers (*larutan penyahjangkit*) must be used in a well-ventilated area. Never mix different disinfectant products — chemical reactions may release toxic fumes. Wear disposable gloves (*sarung tangan pakai buang*) when handling cleaning chemicals and change gloves between clients to prevent cross-contamination (*jangkitan silang*).\n\n### 2.1 Salon Hygiene Standards\n\nHygiene (*kebersihan*) in the salon is governed by:\n- **Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (OSHA)** — Akta Keselamatan dan Kesihatan Pekerjaan 1994\n- **Local authority regulations** — Peraturan pihak berkuasa tempatan\n- **Ministry of Health guidelines** — Garis panduan Kementerian Kesihatan\n\nKey hygiene practices include:\n\n| Practice | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Surface disinfection (*penyahjangkitan permukaan*) | All surfaces wiped with approved disinfectant before and after each client |\n| Hand hygiene (*kebersihan tangan*) | Hand washing with antibacterial soap; use of hand sanitiser |\n| Linen management (*pengurusan linen*) | Fresh towels, gowns and sheets for each client; soiled linen in covered bins |\n| Air quality (*kualiti udara*) | Adequate ventilation; air purifier if required |\n| Floor cleanliness (*kebersihan lantai*) | Swept and mopped daily; no debris or spillage |\n\n### 2.2 Safety Practices\n\n- Ensure no wet floors or tripping hazards in the consultation area.\n- Electrical equipment must be PAT (Portable Appliance Testing) compliant.\n- Fire extinguisher accessible within the salon premises.\n- Emergency exits clearly marked.\n- First aid kit (*peti pertolongan cemas*) available and stocked.\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Tools, Equipment and Materials (Peralatan, Kelengkapan dan Bahan)\n\n### 3.1 Tools (Peralatan)","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":136}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_018A5iwcTM4YgJd3me7iuve5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014UhYFzekucxidMxyMgFqdc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C02\\KP-01.md","limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":997,"cache_read_input_tokens":40907,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":997},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"17fb90c9-0f5e-4e23-b15a-f4e457dc3fd4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014UhYFzekucxidMxyMgFqdc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C02/KP(1/7)\n4\t**Competency Unit:** C02 — Body Thermal Therapy / Terapi Haba Badan\n5\t**Work Activity 1:** Carry Out Body Thermal Therapy Consultation\n6\t**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Objectives (Objektif)\n11\t\n12\tUpon completion of this information sheet, trainees shall be able to:\n13\t1. Explain the process of recording client personal profiles for thermal therapy per the Data Protection Act.\n14\t2. Identify contraindications specific to body thermal therapy.\n15\t3. Describe how to determine client thermal therapy service requirements.\n16\t4. Explain the importance of informed consent for thermal therapy.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 1.0 Introduction (Pengenalan)\n21\t\n22\tBody thermal therapy (*terapi haba badan*) uses heat to induce perspiration (*peluh*), emulsify adipose fats and lipids, reduce fluid retention, promote detoxification (*detoksifikasi*) and improve blood circulation (*peredaran darah*). Before any thermal therapy can be performed, a thorough consultation must be conducted to ensure the client is suitable for heat-based treatments.\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 2.0 Client Personal Profile for Thermal Therapy\n27\t\n28\tBuilding on the general body consultation (C01), the thermal therapy consultation must additionally record:\n29\t\n30\t| Additional Data | Purpose |\n31\t|----------------|---------|\n32\t| Heat tolerance / sensitivity (*toleransi haba*) | Determine if client can withstand heat applications |\n33\t| Circulatory conditions | Assess risk of vasodilation effects |\n34\t| Blood pressure status (current reading if possible) | Hypotension/hypertension risk during heat exposure |\n35\t| Medication affecting heat response | Beta-blockers, vasodilators, anticoagulants |\n36\t| Previous thermal therapy experience | Gauge client familiarity and any past adverse reactions |\n37\t| Skin sensitivity to heat | History of heat rash, burns, photosensitivity |\n38\t\n39\tAll data recorded per the Personal Data Protection Act 2010 (PDPA).\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## 3.0 Contraindications for Body Thermal Therapy\n44\t\n45\t> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Thermal therapy poses significant risk to clients with cardiovascular, circulatory, or skin conditions. NEVER proceed without completing a full contraindication screening (*tapisan kontraindikasi haba*). If there is any doubt about a client's suitability, STOP and obtain written medical clearance (*pelepasan perubatan bertulis*) from a registered medical practitioner before treatment.\n46\t\n47\t### 3.1 Absolute Contraindications (Treatment MUST NOT proceed)\n48\t\n49\t| Contraindication | Reason |\n50\t|-----------------|--------|\n51\t| Heart conditions / cardiovascular disease (*penyakit jantung*) | Heat increases heart rate and vasodilation; risk of cardiac event |\n52\t| Thrombosis / deep vein thrombosis (*trombosis vena dalam*) | Heat may dislodge blood clots |\n53\t| Severe hypertension (*hipertensi teruk*) | Risk of stroke or cardiac emergency |\n54\t| Fever / acute infection (*demam / jangkitan akut*) | Body already under thermal stress |\n55\t| Skin infections / contagious conditions | Risk of spread; heat exacerbates infection |\n56\t| Epilepsy (uncontrolled) | Risk of seizure in heated environment |\n57\t| Cancer / malignancy | Heat may promote metastasis |\n58\t| Recent surgery (< 6 months) | Risk to healing tissues |\n59\t| Open wounds / burns | Heat aggravates damaged tissue |\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"d6171d39-2edd-4311-9e23-ddedab13eb4c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:32:59.828Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C02\\KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C02/KP(1/7)\n**Competency Unit:** C02 — Body Thermal Therapy / Terapi Haba Badan\n**Work Activity 1:** Carry Out Body Thermal Therapy Consultation\n**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n\n---\n\n## Objectives (Objektif)\n\nUpon completion of this information sheet, trainees shall be able to:\n1. Explain the process of recording client personal profiles for thermal therapy per the Data Protection Act.\n2. Identify contraindications specific to body thermal therapy.\n3. Describe how to determine client thermal therapy service requirements.\n4. Explain the importance of informed consent for thermal therapy.\n\n---\n\n## 1.0 Introduction (Pengenalan)\n\nBody thermal therapy (*terapi haba badan*) uses heat to induce perspiration (*peluh*), emulsify adipose fats and lipids, reduce fluid retention, promote detoxification (*detoksifikasi*) and improve blood circulation (*peredaran darah*). Before any thermal therapy can be performed, a thorough consultation must be conducted to ensure the client is suitable for heat-based treatments.\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 Client Personal Profile for Thermal Therapy\n\nBuilding on the general body consultation (C01), the thermal therapy consultation must additionally record:\n\n| Additional Data | Purpose |\n|----------------|---------|\n| Heat tolerance / sensitivity (*toleransi haba*) | Determine if client can withstand heat applications |\n| Circulatory conditions | Assess risk of vasodilation effects |\n| Blood pressure status (current reading if possible) | Hypotension/hypertension risk during heat exposure |\n| Medication affecting heat response | Beta-blockers, vasodilators, anticoagulants |\n| Previous thermal therapy experience | Gauge client familiarity and any past adverse reactions |\n| Skin sensitivity to heat | History of heat rash, burns, photosensitivity |\n\nAll data recorded per the Personal Data Protection Act 2010 (PDPA).\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Contraindications for Body Thermal Therapy\n\n> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Thermal therapy poses significant risk to clients with cardiovascular, circulatory, or skin conditions. NEVER proceed without completing a full contraindication screening (*tapisan kontraindikasi haba*). If there is any doubt about a client's suitability, STOP and obtain written medical clearance (*pelepasan perubatan bertulis*) from a registered medical practitioner before treatment.\n\n### 3.1 Absolute Contraindications (Treatment MUST NOT proceed)\n\n| Contraindication | Reason |\n|-----------------|--------|\n| Heart conditions / cardiovascular disease (*penyakit jantung*) | Heat increases heart rate and vasodilation; risk of cardiac event |\n| Thrombosis / deep vein thrombosis (*trombosis vena dalam*) | Heat may dislodge blood clots |\n| Severe hypertension (*hipertensi teruk*) | Risk of stroke or cardiac emergency |\n| Fever / acute infection (*demam / jangkitan akut*) | Body already under thermal stress |\n| Skin infections / contagious conditions | Risk of spread; heat exacerbates infection |\n| Epilepsy (uncontrolled) | Risk of seizure in heated environment |\n| Cancer / malignancy | Heat may promote metastasis |\n| Recent surgery (< 6 months) | Risk to healing tissues |\n| Open wounds / burns | Heat aggravates damaged tissue |\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":138}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EmeqDoKRXW8xgrwXrHXsgf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough context. Let me form my plan:\n\n**Phase 1 — PLAN:**\n\n1. Create `aesthetic-services/00-aesthetic-terminology.md` — a comprehensive bilingual terminology glossary with 80+ EN/BM term pairs organized by category (anatomy, treatments, equipment, modalities, hygiene) with pronunciation guides for technical BM terms.\n\n2. Create `_agents/validate-aesthetic-terms.py` — a Python validator script (~130 lines) that:\n   - Scans all CU-01 through CU-04 KP documents\n   - Identifies terms with multiple translations\n   - Generates CSV report with inconsistencies\n   - Returns exit code 0 when all KP files pass; exit code 1 with detailed report\n\n**Acceptance Criteria Mapping:**\n1. Glossary with 80+ term pairs, organized by 5 categories, with pronunciation guides → `00-aesthetic-terminology.md`\n2. Python validator scanning CU-01 through CU-04 KP documents, identifies inconsistent terms, generates CSV → `validate-aesthetic-terms.py`\n3. Exit codes 0/1 based on consistency check → implemented in validator script\n\nNow I need to examine the existing KP files to see what EN/BM terminology is already in use.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2283,"cache_read_input_tokens":41904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2283},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"d440946a-e5db-4668-acd5-07a8e00d184d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EmeqDoKRXW8xgrwXrHXsgf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Current State\nAesthetic Services has KP files in C01-C04 with extensive bilingual (EN/BM) terminology already embedded inline throughout the documents. No consolidated glossary exists and no validator script exists.\n\n## Problem Identified\nUS-014 requires: (1) a comprehensive 80+ term bilingual glossary `aesthetic-services/00-aesthetic-terminology.md`, and (2) a Python validator `_agents/validate-aesthetic-terms.py` that scans KP files for inconsistent EN/BM usage and outputs a CSV report.\n\n## Planned Changes\n- Create `aesthetic-services/00-aesthetic-terminology.md` — 80+ EN/BM pairs in 5 categories (anatomy, treatments, equipment, modalities, hygiene) with pronunciation guides\n- Create `_agents/validate-aesthetic-terms.py` — regex-based scanner that extracts EN/BM pairs, detects inconsistencies, writes CSV, exits 0 (pass) or 1 (fail)\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [\"aesthetic-services/00-aesthetic-terminology.md\", \"_agents/validate-aesthetic-terms.py\"],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [\"load_glossary\", \"extract_terms_from_files\", \"find_inconsistencies\", \"write_csv_report\", \"main\"],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 210\n}\n</plan>\n\nLet me read a couple more KP files to harvest terms for the glossary."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2283,"cache_read_input_tokens":41904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2283},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"ad58c135-3297-4167-abea-b6fee4680162"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EmeqDoKRXW8xgrwXrHXsgf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GJ4kUTigdA57HPiRQh7w3t","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-01.md","limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2283,"cache_read_input_tokens":41904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2283},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"edca3c24-8c91-40d9-b1d2-9bc8b33e35e3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJ4kUTigdA57HPiRQh7w3t","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(1/6)\n4\t**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n5\t**Work Activity 1:** Carry Out Manual Body Massage Consultation\n6\t**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Objectives (Objektif)\n11\t1. Explain the consultation process specific to manual body massage.\n12\t2. Identify contraindications for manual body massage.\n13\t3. Describe the informed consent requirements for massage services.\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1.0 Massage Consultation\n18\t\n19\tBuilding on C01 body consultation, the massage consultation additionally determines:\n20\t- Client's primary reason for massage (relaxation, pain relief, circulation improvement, muscle tension release, cellulite reduction)\n21\t- Areas of concern / pain points\n22\t- Pressure preference (light, medium, firm)\n23\t- Previous massage experience and preferences\n24\t- Specific muscle or joint conditions\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## 2.0 Contraindications for Manual Body Massage\n29\t\n30\t> **AMARAN / WARNING:** NEVER proceed with massage if any absolute contraindication is present. For relative contraindications, obtain written GP/specialist clearance where stated and document on the consultation card before commencing treatment.\n31\t\n32\tContraindications are classified as **systemic** (whole-body; massage must not proceed) or **local** (specific area; massage may proceed but must avoid the site).\n33\t\n34\t### 2.1 Absolute Contraindications (Systemic — No Treatment)\n35\t| Condition | Reason |\n36\t|-----------|--------|\n37\t| Fever / acute infection (*demam / jangkitan akut*) | Risk of spreading infection; body under systemic stress |\n38\t| Contagious skin conditions (*penyakit kulit berjangkit*) | Cross-contamination risk to therapist and other clients |\n39\t| Thrombosis / DVT (*trombosis / trombosis urat dalam*) | Risk of dislodging clot → pulmonary embolism |\n40\t| Cancer / malignancy (without oncologist clearance) | Risk of aiding metastatic spread via lymph stimulation |\n41\t| Recent fractures / sprains (acute stage < 72 hrs) | Risk of further tissue damage |\n42\t| Severe osteoporosis (*osteoporosis teruk*) | Pathological fracture risk under pressure |\n43\t| Acute inflammation / swelling (*keradangan akut*) | Massage increases prostaglandin release; exacerbates condition |\n44\t| Alcohol or drug intoxication (*mabuk alkohol / dadah*) | Impaired pain perception; consent not valid |\n45\t| Anti-coagulant medication (e.g. warfarin, heparin) | Elevated bruising and internal bleeding risk |\n46\t| Acute mental health crisis / psychosis | Inability to give valid consent; unpredictable response |\n47\t\n48\t### 2.2 Local Contraindications (Avoid Area Only)\n49\t| Condition | Action |\n50\t|-----------|--------|\n51\t| Open wounds / burns | Do not massage over affected area |\n52\t| Varicose veins (*varikos vena*) | Avoid direct pressure; work proximal and distal only |\n53\t| Undiagnosed lumps / swellings | Avoid until medically cleared |\n54\t| Bruising (*lebam*) | Avoid bruised area; work surrounding tissue gently |\n55\t| Recent scar tissue (< 6 weeks) | Avoid until scar is fully closed |\n56\t\n57\t### 2.3 Relative Contraindications (Proceed with Caution)\n58\t| Condition | Action |\n59\t|-----------|--------|\n60\t| Pregnancy (*hamil*) | Avoid abdomen in first trimester; use side-lying position; GP approval required |\n61\t| Diabetes (*kencing manis*) | Monitor skin response; reduce pressure; check for neuropathy |\n62\t| Epilepsy (controlled) | Proceed with caution; avoid tapotement; have emergency plan |\n63\t| High/low blood pressure (*tekanan darah tinggi/rendah*) | Monitor; slow gentle movements; avoid sudden position changes |\n64\t| Skin conditions (eczema, psoriasis) | Avoid affected areas; use hypoallergenic medium |\n65\t| Recent surgery (> 6 weeks post-op) | Avoid surgical area; require GP clearance |\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 3.0 Informed Consent\n70\t\n71\tThe consent process must include:\n72\t- Explanation of the massage procedure and areas to be treated\n73\t- Expected benefits and possible after-effects (soreness, increased urination)\n74\t- Client's right to stop treatment at any time\n75\t- Confirmation of pressure preference\n76\t- Written consent with signature\n77\t\n78\t---\n79\t\n80\t## 4.0 Recording Consultation Findings"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"ebe7b246-f2fd-4942-a6c4-79a58c04f5de","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:33:14.597Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(1/6)\n**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n**Work Activity 1:** Carry Out Manual Body Massage Consultation\n**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n\n---\n\n## Objectives (Objektif)\n1. Explain the consultation process specific to manual body massage.\n2. Identify contraindications for manual body massage.\n3. Describe the informed consent requirements for massage services.\n\n---\n\n## 1.0 Massage Consultation\n\nBuilding on C01 body consultation, the massage consultation additionally determines:\n- Client's primary reason for massage (relaxation, pain relief, circulation improvement, muscle tension release, cellulite reduction)\n- Areas of concern / pain points\n- Pressure preference (light, medium, firm)\n- Previous massage experience and preferences\n- Specific muscle or joint conditions\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 Contraindications for Manual Body Massage\n\n> **AMARAN / WARNING:** NEVER proceed with massage if any absolute contraindication is present. For relative contraindications, obtain written GP/specialist clearance where stated and document on the consultation card before commencing treatment.\n\nContraindications are classified as **systemic** (whole-body; massage must not proceed) or **local** (specific area; massage may proceed but must avoid the site).\n\n### 2.1 Absolute Contraindications (Systemic — No Treatment)\n| Condition | Reason |\n|-----------|--------|\n| Fever / acute infection (*demam / jangkitan akut*) | Risk of spreading infection; body under systemic stress |\n| Contagious skin conditions (*penyakit kulit berjangkit*) | Cross-contamination risk to therapist and other clients |\n| Thrombosis / DVT (*trombosis / trombosis urat dalam*) | Risk of dislodging clot → pulmonary embolism |\n| Cancer / malignancy (without oncologist clearance) | Risk of aiding metastatic spread via lymph stimulation |\n| Recent fractures / sprains (acute stage < 72 hrs) | Risk of further tissue damage |\n| Severe osteoporosis (*osteoporosis teruk*) | Pathological fracture risk under pressure |\n| Acute inflammation / swelling (*keradangan akut*) | Massage increases prostaglandin release; exacerbates condition |\n| Alcohol or drug intoxication (*mabuk alkohol / dadah*) | Impaired pain perception; consent not valid |\n| Anti-coagulant medication (e.g. warfarin, heparin) | Elevated bruising and internal bleeding risk |\n| Acute mental health crisis / psychosis | Inability to give valid consent; unpredictable response |\n\n### 2.2 Local Contraindications (Avoid Area Only)\n| Condition | Action |\n|-----------|--------|\n| Open wounds / burns | Do not massage over affected area |\n| Varicose veins (*varikos vena*) | Avoid direct pressure; work proximal and distal only |\n| Undiagnosed lumps / swellings | Avoid until medically cleared |\n| Bruising (*lebam*) | Avoid bruised area; work surrounding tissue gently |\n| Recent scar tissue (< 6 weeks) | Avoid until scar is fully closed |\n\n### 2.3 Relative Contraindications (Proceed with Caution)\n| Condition | Action |\n|-----------|--------|\n| Pregnancy (*hamil*) | Avoid abdomen in first trimester; use side-lying position; GP approval required |\n| Diabetes (*kencing manis*) | Monitor skin response; reduce pressure; check for neuropathy |\n| Epilepsy (controlled) | Proceed with caution; avoid tapotement; have emergency plan |\n| High/low blood pressure (*tekanan darah tinggi/rendah*) | Monitor; slow gentle movements; avoid sudden position changes |\n| Skin conditions (eczema, psoriasis) | Avoid affected areas; use hypoallergenic medium |\n| Recent surgery (> 6 weeks post-op) | Avoid surgical area; require GP clearance |\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Informed Consent\n\nThe consent process must include:\n- Explanation of the massage procedure and areas to be treated\n- Expected benefits and possible after-effects (soreness, increased urination)\n- Client's right to stop treatment at any time\n- Confirmation of pressure preference\n- Written consent with signature\n\n---\n\n## 4.0 Recording Consultation Findings","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":94}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01EmeqDoKRXW8xgrwXrHXsgf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018bhm1F7vVWi6s5JyKEFpkc","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C04\\KP-01.md","limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2283,"cache_read_input_tokens":41904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2283},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"667c81a5-8791-4dc0-8ac0-f3d40a40e153"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018bhm1F7vVWi6s5JyKEFpkc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C04/KP(1/10)\n4\t**CU:** C04 — Body Electrotherapy | **WA 1:** Prepare Body Electrotherapy Work Area\n5\t**Paper Colour:** WHITE\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Objectives\n10\t1. Explain personal hygiene and PPE requirements for electrotherapy.\n11\t2. Identify, describe and explain the arrangement of electrotherapy tools, equipment and materials.\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 1.0 Personal Hygiene and PPE\n16\t\n17\t| PPE Item | Purpose |\n18\t|----------|---------|\n19\t| Apron (*apron*) | Protect uniform from conductive gels and products |\n20\t| Mask (*topeng muka*) | Protection from aerosols (high frequency) |\n21\t| Disposable gloves | Hygiene during electrode placement and gel application |\n22\t| Closed-toe shoes | Electrical safety; anti-static soles preferred |\n23\t\n24\tTherapist must: wash hands before and after each client, maintain short filed nails, remove metallic jewellery (electrical conduction risk).\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## 2.0 Electrotherapy Equipment Overview\n29\t\n30\t| Equipment | Function | Key Parameters |\n31\t|-----------|----------|---------------|\n32\t| **Faradic/EMS unit** (*unit faradik/EMS*) | Passive muscle contraction via interrupted direct current | Frequency, pulse width, intensity |\n33\t| **Galvanic unit** (*unit galvanik*) | Iontophoresis (product penetration) and desincrustation (deep cleansing) | Polarity (anode/cathode), intensity (mA) |\n34\t| **Vacuum suction machine** (*mesin sedutan vakum*) | Lymphatic drainage, cellulite reduction | Suction pressure, cup size |\n35\t| **Mechanical/gyratory massager** (*pengurut mekanikal*) | Mechanical massage using rotating applicators | Speed, applicator head type |\n36\t| **High frequency unit** (*unit frekuensi tinggi*) | Stimulate circulation, germicidal effect (direct/indirect methods) | Electrode type, intensity |\n37\t| **Micro-current unit** (*unit arus mikro*) | Muscle re-education, skin tightening, anti-ageing | Current (microamps), frequency, waveform |\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 3.0 Equipment Arrangement\n42\t\n43\tArrange electrotherapy area in treatment flow:\n44\t1. Client preparation zone — couch, trolley with preparation products\n45\t2. Treatment zone — electrotherapy equipment positioned within arm's reach\n46\t3. Product zone — conductive gels, electrode pads, covers\n47\t4. Clean-up zone — waste bins, sanitising products\n48\t\n49\tEquipment should be on stable, non-conductive surfaces. Cords organized to prevent tripping.\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t## 4.0 Pre-Session Checks\n54\t\n55\t1. Inspect all equipment cables for damage.\n56\t2. Check plugs and connections are secure.\n57\t3. Test each machine on low setting before client use.\n58\t4. Verify earth/ground connections are intact.\n59\t5. Check electrode pads/applicators for wear and hygiene.\n60\t6. Record equipment check in the log book."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"683f9d01-fafb-4ba2-bc2d-1235d039a90c","uuid":"2706c65b-a48f-45f3-958d-5ceea58595a4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:33:14.974Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C04\\KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C04/KP(1/10)\n**CU:** C04 — Body Electrotherapy | **WA 1:** Prepare Body Electrotherapy Work Area\n**Paper Colour:** WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Objectives\n1. Explain personal hygiene and PPE requirements for electrotherapy.\n2. Identify, describe and explain the arrangement of electrotherapy tools, equipment and materials.\n\n---\n\n## 1.0 Personal Hygiene and PPE\n\n| PPE Item | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| Apron (*apron*) | Protect uniform from conductive gels and products |\n| Mask (*topeng muka*) | Protection from aerosols (high frequency) |\n| Disposable gloves | Hygiene during electrode placement and gel application |\n| Closed-toe shoes | Electrical safety; anti-static soles preferred |\n\nTherapist must: wash hands before and after each client, maintain short filed nails, remove metallic jewellery (electrical conduction risk).\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 Electrotherapy Equipment Overview\n\n| Equipment | Function | Key Parameters |\n|-----------|----------|---------------|\n| **Faradic/EMS unit** (*unit faradik/EMS*) | Passive muscle contraction via interrupted direct current | Frequency, pulse width, intensity |\n| **Galvanic unit** (*unit galvanik*) | Iontophoresis (product penetration) and desincrustation (deep cleansing) | Polarity (anode/cathode), intensity (mA) |\n| **Vacuum suction machine** (*mesin sedutan vakum*) | Lymphatic drainage, cellulite reduction | Suction pressure, cup size |\n| **Mechanical/gyratory massager** (*pengurut mekanikal*) | Mechanical massage using rotating applicators | Speed, applicator head type |\n| **High frequency unit** (*unit frekuensi tinggi*) | Stimulate circulation, germicidal effect (direct/indirect methods) | Electrode type, intensity |\n| **Micro-current unit** (*unit arus mikro*) | Muscle re-education, skin tightening, anti-ageing | Current (microamps), frequency, waveform |\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Equipment Arrangement\n\nArrange electrotherapy area in treatment flow:\n1. Client preparation zone — couch, trolley with preparation products\n2. Treatment zone — electrotherapy equipment positioned within arm's reach\n3. Product zone — conductive gels, electrode pads, covers\n4. Clean-up zone — waste bins, sanitising products\n\nEquipment should be on stable, non-conductive surfaces. Cords organized to prevent tripping.\n\n---\n\n## 4.0 Pre-Session Checks\n\n1. Inspect all equipment cables for damage.\n2. Check plugs and connections are secure.\n3. Test each machine on low setting before client use.\n4. Verify earth/ground connections are intact.\n5. Check electrode pads/applicators for wear and hygiene.\n6. Record equipment check in the log book.","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}}}
